ng activities and we  think we're learning and in fact  it just seems to slip through our  fingers within a few days of all of the  you know declensions in russian or  whatever might be have just simply  evaporated  so so that's always been my prejudice  and occasionally i stray from that so  here i am learning hebrew  struggling to  master the hebrew alphabet  where  some of the symbols are completely  different from anything i've seen some  of the symbols resemble you know like an  eye  in english or in the latin alphabet and  yet it's sometimes  sometimes ooh sometimes in and it's very  difficult to get the brain to adapt to  this new  system  and so where i've daily been trying to  read and struggling to read and  struggling to remember what the  meaning of of these significance of  these letters  was i have now adopted a new technique  which i'm going to share with you and  that is to say  that because i study  on my ipad and i have my  many stories here  and of course we have text-to-speech so  i can now read the story  uh  what i've started doing now is i go  through every story i just go through it  and i hear you see  you tell me  and so i with every word that i i press  on with my stylus of course i get the  meaning and i get the sound  so i don't struggle to try and read this  word it tells me what the word is if i  saw that i'm not trying to like to me  this looks like an i o but it's not with  with two little quotation marks but it's  not  it's your c it's not i o it's your c so  you know by doing this often enough it's  going to naturally flow into my brain  that pattern gets sort of established  and it's a lot more pleasant i don't  have to fight it i don't have to  struggle i just go through the lesson  when i'm finished  i listen to the natural voice and i  follow along again  so that i'm giving myself this sort of  more natural exposure to the language  word for word  if the language were written say in an  alphabet that i was more familiar with i  think i would do phrases  but still the idea of going through the  lesson once hearing it in text to speech  whether it be word for word or phrase by  phrase and then again listening to the  human voice read that again and you read  along  is is i'm not forcing myself to do  anything i'm just letting it flow in i'm  spending time with the language  which again is a key you know attitude  spending time with the language and i'm  very suspicious of  learning activities which force you to  think  yeah when they you know  you have quizzes and drills i mentioned  the other day living language tries to  get you to match up these new hebrew  words that supposedly you've learned and  that match them to the corresponding  english word as if this exercise in  forcing you to try and remember  something  is going to have some benefit i it has  not been my experience i i think it  won't have any benefit i'm better off to  spend that time just letting more of the  language wash over me and because in  hebrew of course  getting used to the writing system is is  such a big  hurdle initially and i want to get to  where i can read so that i can listen  and read and follow my normal  learning practices  so i would rather spend my time just  letting the language wash over me and  that's why i think this approach of  going through these lessons just going  through them uh  knocking them off the list there's now  30 mini stories in hebrew i'm going to  go through all 30 mini stories and go  back again go back again i can go back  again with a different objective  you know and i am  some words for you know  preposition or  um  whatever it might be uh you know  adjective adverb  time  you know verbs  but initially i just want to let it wash  over me and so this is an approach and  it's kind of more of a  more of this passive uh  learning as opposed to deliberate study  so i would be interested in hearing from  you how effective is deliberate study  how effective is forcing you to think  uh as opposed to simply allowing you  know exposing yourself to more and more  of the language letting it wash over you  and letting the brain gradually start to  form some patterns  so there you have it deliberate language  study versus a more natural approach to  acquiring the language i would be  interested in hearing your views thank  you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

